well this week's the castama described  the conservative at leadership race as a  traveling circus he also criticized the  candidates for promising billions of  pounds of unfunded tax cuts laborers  chosen to focus on tax cuts of a  different kind with a pledge to get rid  of the charitable status held by some  private schools and then reinvest that  money in State education well the shadow  education secretary Bridget Phillipson  joins us now thank you for being with us  um I just want to start off by talking  about the Heat Wave and it's something  that so many of our viewers are going to  be really concerned about and the  government is Keen to keep schools open  we spoke to Dominic Rob earlier on the  program who said it's important that  children don't miss school  what's your position on this would you  support schools that do decide to close  in the hoop I think head teachers are  usually the best people to decide what's  right in the particular circumstances of  their school and you know closing  schools should be a last resort of  course it should be I think what we've  seen however over the last 12 years is a  failure to get our schools into the best  possible state to deal with these kinds  of problems that applies when you have  very hot weather but also applies where  you have very cold weather and we've got  a crumbling schools estate and a  government that's failed to invest over  the last 12 years in making sure that  our schools are fit for the future a lot  of parents will be you know facing the  decision about whether to send their  children into school on Monday or  Tuesday particularly Wednesday and when  the heat is so high and would you  support parents who decide to keep their  children off school on those days I'm  sure that head teachers will be doing  everything they possibly can to make  sure that their schools kept as cool as  possible that children are kept out of  the hot sun and are kept indoors as much  as possible I think children have missed  out quite a lot already in terms of  their education and it's right for them  to be there trust their teacher  basically okay they will always act in  the best interests of the children in  school  um I we just spoke to mcwheelan a moment  ago who of course is the head of the  aslav Union at one of a number of unions  that who are going on strike a due back  the train driver strikes we don't want  the strikes to go ahead we didn't last  time and I would certainly want further  strikes to be avoided because whenever  you get to a position where you have a  strike that represents a failure of all  of the parties to reach an agreed  position what we saw with the transport  Secretary of course is that he refused  consistently to get around the table to  try and find a way through it because  this is about you know cost of living  about the rising pressures that workers  are facing and also about safety on our  Railways I mean whether there's any  question now given the chaos that we're  seeing in government about the transport  circuit or whoever that might be in a  couple of weeks doing the right thing  and being responsible in terms of  getting around the table I think that  remains to be seen it's interesting I've  spoken to quite a lot of union leaders  over the last few weeks and it's a  pretty consistent message about Labor  they're frustrated effectively their  labor isn't doing more to support them  in strong action they say that labor  needs to demonstrate that it's a party  that's on the side of working people can  you understand that frustration I  understand the pressures that Working  Families and workers across a range of  different parts of our economy are  feeling right now we know that wages  have been stagnant for many many years  we know that everything is more  expensive and that that means really  hard times for people that do incredibly  important jobs whether that's our  teachers staff on the railways I mean I  think what the trade Unions would  recognize is that from government labor  had a really strong record of making  sure that we did support all of our  staff in those key Industries and that's  what we do again  um you mentioned teachers there who of  course have seen you know pay screens  for many years now with austerity as  well teachers are considering strike  action would you support them in that  well I really hope it doesn't come to  that as I understand it the review body  will be presenting to ministers what the  what their recommendations are I'm not  going to cut across that process but I  think as you say when when you look at  the real terms pay cuts that teachers  have had over the last 12 years the  pressure that they're under the fact  they've worked so hard during the  pandemic and have not been recognized by  the government I think it's also  important however to mention School  support staff who have had you know a  really difficult time are so important  to our children getting the support that  they need in school and of course one of  the leadership candidates was incredibly  dismissive talked about Superfluous  support staff I don't know what plant  she's living on if she thinks we've got  too many stuff in our school supporting  childrenock was very dismissive of our  school support staff but right across  you know Nadeem zahar with a new  Chancellor the former education  secretary he's been saying we've had 20  cuts across government departments I  think that extends to education I think  that he was clarifying the 20 cut was to  civil servants not not the spending on  all government departments well that has  a massive impact I mean who does he  think will be responsible for delivering  on government priorities at the moment  for example I'm inundated with people  who can't get a pass sport to go on  holiday who does he think is responsible  for making sure that people across the  country can get a passport and get can  get a well-deserved break we were  talking about teachers pay and there  were reports that the body that you're  talking about are going to recommend a  five percent pay rise which of course  would end up being another real terms  pay cut if you look at inflation as well  and how much would a labor government  increase Teachers Pay by well let's wait  and see what they say we don't know that  yet I'm not going to put a figure on  that and I'm not going to cut across  that negotiating process the level of  pay and the figure that you put on it is  incredibly important but I know from  speaking to teachers and support staff  as well that there are bigger questions  too around workload and that also needs  addressing I guess I guess I understand  you don't want to cut across blah blah  but at the same time what is the policy  from labor what would be a reasonable  pay rise for teachers well if you look  at our record from government we  delivered consistent stable pay Rises  for teachers and for support I'm not  going to ask about the record I'm asking  about what would be your policy you want  to be the education secretary what what  would be a reasonable pay recipe well I  think you do have to judge us on our  record if I were education secretary I  would be working with trade unions  representing teachers and support staff  to make sure that they are properly  supported both in terms of pay and in  terms of workload that is also why we've  set out that we would end the tax breaks  private schools enjoy we think that's  completely wrong and out of step and  that we would put that money into making  sure that all of our children in our  brilliant State schools get the best  possible start in life okay  um just to quickly End by talking a  little bit about the concept of party  leadership election what content would  you be most worried about fighting in a  general election I'm not worried about  any of them  I don't think we've got anything to fear  from any of the candidates and the  reason for that is that all of them have  propped up Boris Johnson and the  conservative government for 12 years now  and over those 12 years what have we  seen happen in our country we've seen  our Vital Public Services whether that's  schools or hospitals get worse and worse  Britain is completely stuck you can't  renew your passport more and more people  go into food banks Rising levels of  child poverty growth at a terrible level  a consistent failure to grow our economy  and what are they all doing an arms race  around tax cuts none of them prepared to  set out what that means how many  teachers how many doctors what will be  the impact on our Public Services they  should be asked those questions around  what that means and how they will  deliver it labor however we've been  clear that when we put forward any  policy we'll be clear about how we  funded so today we are saying we are  reiterating our call for the government  to end those tax breaks the private  schools enjoy and to put that money into  State education will any of those  candidates make that commitment okay  thank you very much indeed Bridget  Phillips in there for the labor party  here on the show today

